.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-stage-for-location 1  "tapectl-stage-for-location " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-stage\-for\-location \- Stage what a location needs, packed into one volume bound there
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl stage for\-location\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] <\fB\-\-volume\fR> [\fB\-\-jobs\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fILOCATION\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stage what a location needs, packed into one volume bound there
.PP
Works out each unit\*(Aqs need at LOCATION (ADR\-0012 items 151\-159): group 1, its copy target there is unmet; group 2, it is short of its copy floor with no target there and no copy there yet. A new or changed unit counts as a new version with no copies. The units are packed into `\-\-volume`\*(Aqs room in priority order (the largest shortfall at LOCATION first, the fewest copies in all breaking ties, then the name), whole units before units that may span, next\-fit: the first that does not fit closes the tape, and the rest wait for the next tape for LOCATION. What is already staged for LOCATION is planned first and never staged again. A span in progress continues only on a tape bound for the location of its earlier range.
.PP
New and changed units are snapshotted and staged, an unchanged unit\*(Aqs version is staged from its source, and a tape\-only unit\*(Aqs version is read back with `volume read\-slices`: the commands are printed in the order to run them, and this command moves no tape. Then write the volume with `volume write LABEL \-\-for\-location LOCATION`. `\-\-dry\-run` prints the plan and stages nothing. A warehouse location is refused: a copy there is a recorded deposit (`volume deposit add`).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-volume\fR \fI<LABEL>\fR
The volume to fill: initialized, nothing written yet
.TP
\fB\-\-jobs\fR \fI<JOBS>\fR
How many units to stage at once (1 to 16; default `[staging] jobs`)

As `stage create \-\-jobs`.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The drive whose fill ceiling and reserve size the volume\*(Aqs room

No drive is touched. Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fILOCATION\fR>
Where the volume will be kept: a registered location, not a warehouse
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
